WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.183 --> 00:00:06.600
RYAN MATSUMOTO:
Cloud Shell Editor

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.240
makes it easy for you to run
code from your web browser

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.800
and deploy it to the Cloud.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.510
And Google Kubernetes
Engine makes

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.940
it easy to deploy,
manage, and scale

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.540
your containerized applications
using Google infrastructure.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.880
So you might be
wondering, can I write

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.130
the code for my Google
Kubernetes Engine apps

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.630
in Cloud Shell Editor?

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.170
Yes, you definitely can.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.820
With this Cloud
code integration,

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.770
Cloud Shell Editor
gives you the power

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.140
to write code for your
Kubernetes application,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.470
test and debug the app locally,
create Google Kubernetes Engine

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.830
clusters, and finally deploy
your app to a cluster.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.570
In this video,
we're going to learn

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.630
how to use Cloud Shell Editor to
deploy an application to Google

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:45.830
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.420
Let's go.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.270
First, visit
ide.cloud.google.com

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.880
to launch the
Cloud Shell Editor.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.870
On the status bar at the
bottom of your screen,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.710
click Cloud Code.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.170
From the Cloud Code menu,
click New Application,

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.986
and then select
Kubernetes Application

00:01:01.986 --> 00:01:04.069
for the type of sample app
that we want to create.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.402
After that, we'll get a list
of Google Kubernetes Engine

00:01:06.402 --> 00:01:08.280
samples to choose from.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.010
Let's go with the Go
Hello, World option.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.590
Next, select a folder in
your Cloud Shell environment

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.980
to save your application
to and give it a name.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.850
If that looks good, click
Create New Application.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.310
Cloud Shell Editor loads the
files for your sample app

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.780
into a new workspace.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.510
You can see the list of files
in the Explorer panel located

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.380
on the left side of the screen.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.643
Now let's go ahead and
test our app locally.

00:01:30.643 --> 00:01:32.810
For this next section, you
will run your application

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.600
on a local Kubernetes
cluster in Cloud Shell.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.200
In the top menu, click
Terminal, and then New Terminal.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.660
This opens up a new Cloud
Shell Terminal window

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.180
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.120
In your Terminal,
run Mini Cube Start

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.210
to start your local
mini cube cluster.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.010
Once your cluster
is set up, you will

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.080
see a confirmation message.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.968
From the Cloud Code menu,
select Run on Kubernetes,

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:59.260
and confirm that you want to
use the mini cube context.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:03.895
The Output panel
displays the progress

00:02:03.895 --> 00:02:05.270
as your app is
built and deployed

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.590
to the local mini cube cluster.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.270
Once your app is built,
you can launch it

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:14.240
by hovering over the URL link
displayed in the Output panel

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.920
and clicking Open Web Preview.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.530
Congratulations.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.990
If you see this page, that
means you've successfully

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.360
run your first app on
Kubernetes using Cloud Code.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.970
Now let's go ahead
and modify the app.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.800
To learn more about
this Hello World app,

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.560
you can browse the files
in the Explorer tab.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.440
At a high level, it includes
a Go Hello World web

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.550
app in main.go that returns a
templated It's Running response

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.850
to all received requests.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.410
There's also a load
balancer service

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.700
called Go Hello, World
External in hello.service.YAML

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.970
that exposes the app by
describing a Kubernetes

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:54.540
service.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.090
Now let's go ahead
and modify the app.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.940
Select the main.go
file and replace It's

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.460
Running with It's Redeploying.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.100
The file saves and
rebuilds automatically.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.250
You can monitor your app's
progress in the Output panel.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.090
This is Cloud Code's Watch mode.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.090
But it's something you
can turn off if you want.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.860
Once your app finishes
building and deploying,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.840
refresh your open tab
to see the updated app.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.880
To analyze your app
while it's running,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.610
use the log viewer
to monitor its logs.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.540
Open the Command Palette by
selecting View, Find Command.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.827
Type Cloud Code View
Logs and click on it.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.430
This opens up the
Log Viewer, which

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.150
allows you to filter and
navigate the logs for your app.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.560
In the Deployment section,
select Go Hello, World.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.060
Now refresh your
app in the browser.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.520
Come back to the Log Viewer
and click the Logs Refresh

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.590
button to see new logs.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:02.260
You should see a new message
for the latest Get request.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.460
So far, you've been
running your app locally.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.180
Now it's time to push your
app to a remote cluster.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.910
First, you'll need to create
a new Google Kubernetes Engine

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.740
cluster to deploy your app to.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.630
Click on the Cloud
Code Kubernetes

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:16.000
icon in the Navigation bar.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.519
This Kubernetes Explorer
makes Kubernetes development

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.700
easier by simplifying cluster
creation and management.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.650
Hover over the
Kubernetes Explorer bar,

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:24.970
and click on the plus sign.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:28.240
Select Create GKE Cluster.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.875
In the Create Cluster Wizard,
select your Google Cloud

00:04:30.875 --> 00:04:31.375
project.

00:04:34.032 --> 00:04:36.240
You'll need to select some
properties for the cluster

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.860
you're going to launch, such
as the zone or region, cluster

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.600
name, and any other selections.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:47.965
Click Create Cluster.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:52.840
Cluster creation may
take a few minutes.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.610
But once it's
created, your cluster

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.990
is listed within the
Kubernetes Explorer view.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:02.650
Finally, let's deploy your
app to the new GKE cluster.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.620
From the Cloud Code menu,
select Run on Kubernetes.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.880
Confirm your newly
created cluster

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.700
as the context for
your deployment.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.860
Confirm the default option
for your image registry.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:20.870
Once your app is
successfully deployed,

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.230
you can launch it via the link
displayed in your Output panel.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:25.340
Congratulations.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.210
You've just created and run your
first Kubernetes app on GKE.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.150
Don't forget to delete your
cluster using the GKE Explorer

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:35.510
to avoid any unwanted charges.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.880
Just right click your cluster
and select Delete Cluster.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.922
Want to try this out yourself?

00:05:43.922 --> 00:05:45.630
Check out the interactive
tutorial linked

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.347
in the description below.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:48.930
Make sure to subscribe
to the channel,

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.490
and stay tuned for more
Cloud Shell Editor videos.

